        <section class="content-panel prose" aria-labelledby="developer-delivery-foundations">
            <h2 id="developer-delivery-foundations">
                Developer and Delivery Foundations
            </h2>
            <p>
                I developed a shared developer toolbox with Dev Containers, Docker/Podman Compose, and PowerShell and Bash setup and teardown scripts. Consumer-repository bootstrap tooling adds shared support while preserving existing files.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    A repeatable local service baseline supplies database, cache, storage, messaging, email, API-mocking, and telemetry resources.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Local developer services use Compose-based orchestration with Docker or Podman. The separate QA validation environment manages dependency-aware startup and cleanup for resources it owns.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The toolbox brings together curated engineering instructions, roles, prompts, and skills, with synchronized adapters for GitHub Copilot, Codex, and Claude Code.
                </li>
                <li>
                    For the QA platform, package delivery separates build, contract validation, container acceptance, and publication. Downstream validation checks the producing build and artifact hashes before using the binaries.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                These delivered resources complement my pipeline implementation and release-management experience below. Delivery templates provide reusable starting points; downstream configuration and validation remain part of adoption. <a asp-page="/Experience/EmploymentAccomplishments" asp-fragment="developer-enablement">Read the developer enablement accomplishments</a>, <a asp-page="/Skills/DeveloperEnablement">engineering enablement practices</a>, and <a asp-page="/Skills/Containers">Docker and Podman architecture</a>.
            </p>
        </section>
        <section class="content-panel prose">
            <h2 id="administrative-experience">
                Administrative Experience
            </h2>
            <p>
                My first experience with TFS was in 2011 while working on contract with the LDS Church. I learned how to create and manage
                builds and even added Continuous Deployment to our Development / Test lanes for the project that we were developing. I
                gained a solid understanding of the Team Foundation Version Control (TFVC) repository system including the practice and process
                for proper branching and merging changes within TFVC.
            </p>
            <p>
                After my contract I went to work for SwipeClock. While I was there I assisted in the adoption and implementation of TFS on premise.
                I helped with team and project creation including the configuration of continuous deployment to our development servers. During this
                time, I also functioned as a TFS Administrator for the system and Release Manager, however the majority of my time was focused on
                writing code as one of the company’s Sr. Developers.
            </p>
            <p>
                I then went back on contact to work for the LDS Church and while on the team I was the project administrator for our TFS instance and
                functioned as the Release Manager. It was one of my focus areas in helping our entire team migrate from JIRA to TFS. We had been
                using TFS for our code repository but we were not using the project management capabilities of the system. One of the biggest advantages
                that we wanted was the ability to track what work was completed with what code was checked into the repository.
                After the migration we added GIT in addition to our TFVC repository. After having worked with both Azure DevOps (VSTS / TFS) and JIRA I feel that the
                TFS/VSTS environment is a better wholistic enterprise solution for any IT team.
            </p>
            <p>
                While working for Academy Mortgage, it was decided by business to bring in Azure DevOps (VSTS / TFS) and evaluate its use
                for our teams. Azure DevOps is the Azure cloud version of VSTS / TFS, a SaaS based solution for IT and
                Development shops. My previous experience with TFS was invaluable for the company in evaluating the new solution. Academy was using an
                aging instance of GO / CD / Chef deployment that was taking our DevOps team over 25% of their time just in maintenance of the system and
                services. Working with one of the DevOps team members, we reduced the effort to create an entire deployment pipeline from two to three
                weeks to three or four hours. I can typically create pipelines in hours; their execution time varies with the product and the complexity
                of the build and deployment. I was later promoted to manage the DevOps team and provide best practices and governance over the
                Azure DevOps / VSTS / TFS instance.
            </p>
        </section>
